---
// Airbnb and holiday let cleaning — its own Level-A item in the service band
// (navigation.ts), moved 25 Sep 2026 from /house-cleaning/airbnb-cleaning/ at
// the client's request, with no redirect from the old URL. Formerly a sibling
// of /house-cleaning/real-estate-cleaning/ and /house-cleaning/ndis-cleaning/
// under Home Cleaning's "Specialist cleaning" group.
//
// Built in the same pass as /house-cleaning/real-estate-cleaning/ and sharing its
// structure deliberately, the same call the two commercial leaves made:
// two pages off one menu group that open differently read as two sites.
//
// NO CONTENT BRIEF EXISTS in content-plans/ for this page. But unlike the
// commercial leaves, this one is NOT drafted from nothing — the Brand
// Foundation Document has a full audience section for it (§7 "Audience 2:
// Airbnb and short-stay hosts", plus §3's third differentiator and §5's
// approved headline/CTA sets). Every positioning claim below traces to that
// document, and the places it is quoted are marked. Copy still needs
// sign-off; approved *positioning* is not the same as approved *page copy*.
//
// ✅ UPDATE (September 2026): the supplied copy for §4 and both FAQ blocks
// came without these brackets, so none of the first five is visible on the
// page any more. The list is kept as the record of what was open. Note the
// page still does not SAY how the calendar sync works, who supplies linen, or
// the same-day cut-off; it now simply does not raise them.
//
// Was: BLOCKS LAUNCH — all six are operational facts only TLB holds, and all
// six ship as visible [brackets] per house convention rather than as plausible
// guesses:
//   - HOW the calendar sync actually works. The BFD says TLB "syncs with
//     your booking calendar" and "manages backend scheduling directly
//     around bookings". That is a real differentiator and the page leans on
//     it, but nothing states the mechanism — iCal feed, channel manager, PMS
//     integration, or a person reading the bookings. Naming a specific
//     integration would fabricate a system. Bracketed in §4 and branded FAQ 1.
//   - LINEN. Does TLB supply and launder linen, or turn the host's own? This
//     is the single biggest cost and logistics question in short-stay
//     cleaning and the page cannot answer it. Bracketed in §4 and
//     non-branded FAQ 3.
//   - CONSUMABLES. The BFD promises restocked essentials "such as toilet
//     paper and olive oil". Who buys them, and are they billed on? Bracketed
//     in §4.
//   - SAME-DAY TURNOVER CUT-OFF. The one thing a host rings about. What is
//     the latest checkout TLB can still turn for a same-day check-in, and is
//     there a booking deadline? Bracketed in branded FAQ 2.
//   - PRICING BASIS. Flat rate per turnover or hourly, and is there a
//     minimum? Bracketed in non-branded FAQ 1. A "from $X" that turns out
//     wrong is worse than no number; the ACCC treats from-pricing as a claim.
//   - [insured], inherited from every sibling page. One answer fixes all.
//
// ⚠️ COMPLIANCE READ REQUIRED on non-branded FAQ 5 (short-stay planning
// rules). It describes NSW's short-term rental accommodation framework and
// the Byron Shire day cap. Those rules are live, contested and periodically
// amended, and a host may act on what this page says. It is written without
// dates, precinct lists or a specific commencement on purpose. It needs a
// verification pass and a visible "last reviewed" date before launch, or it
// should come off the page. See that FAQ's own comment.
//
// NOT BUILT, deliberately: hero video, credibility signals, metrics. No
// direction exists for any of them and the deep-cleaning page's precedent is
// that a credibility block filled on an assumption gets removed again.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../components/sections/SiteHeader.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
import TrustBar from '../components/sections/TrustBar.astro';
import Backdrop from '../components/ui/Backdrop.astro';
import TextBlock from '../components/sections/TextBlock.astro';
import PathwayCards from '../components/sections/PathwayCards.astro';
import ServiceBlocks from '../components/sections/ServiceBlocks.astro';
import Callout from '../components/sections/Callout.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../components/sections/BeforeAfter.astro';
// Before/after pairs — real TLB job photos. The `sample-` files are flat
// illustrations and stay only where no real pair exists for that subject yet.
// The content imports sit here, where the four before/after photo imports
// were, on purpose: Astro orders a page's CSS by import position, so moving
// them changes the order of the built stylesheets.
import { localBusiness } from '../data/business';
import { z } from 'astro/zod';
import { getPage } from '../lib/cms';
import * as section from '../lib/cms-schemas';
import { sized } from '../lib/cms-schemas';
import TagCloud from '../components/sections/TagCloud.astro';
import ComparisonTable from '../components/sections/ComparisonTable.astro';
import GoogleReviewSlider from '../components/sections/GoogleReviewSlider.astro';
import { googleBusinessProfileUrl, reviewsFeaturing } from '../data/googleReviews';
import Faq from '../components/sections/Faq.astro';
import CallToAction from '../components/sections/CallToAction.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../components/sections/SiteFooter.astro';
import logoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-horizontal.png';
// This page's own assets, generated per IMAGE-GUIDELINES; the prompts are in
// src/assets/airbnb/IMAGE-PROMPTS.md.
//
// ⚠️ NOT REUSED: the homepage's old holiday-let photo (since deleted).
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES §5 says reuse before generating, and that rule was
// deliberately broken here: that photo was a snowbound English stone cottage
// with a wood burner and bare winter trees through the glass. Not the
// Northern Rivers, not Australia, and the opposite season. On 6 Oct 2026 the
// homepage replaced it everywhere with a real client-supplied hinterland
// photo; see the sheet's opening note.
// The three pathway cards are real TLB photos (6 Oct 2026), not generated.
// The kitchen and bed files are pre-cropped to the cards' 4:3 so the hands
// stay in frame; the shower one is shared with the deep cleaning page. The
// generated frames they replaced still run on the turnover checklist guide.
// §7a "What you get after every stay". Real TLB photos (6 Oct 2026),
// pre-cropped to the cards' 4:3; see the block above afterEveryStayItems.
// §2 backdrop, behind the Cream scrim — the homepage's, same as every sibling.
// Before/after. Both pairs are REAL TLB job photography. See §7.
import footerLogoImg from '../assets/logo/tlb-logo-square.png';
import {
  primaryNav,
  headerNav,
  headerSecondaryCta,
  footerServiceLinks,
  footerContact,
  footerCopyright,
  quoteCta,
} from '../data/navigation';
import { comparisonColumns, comparisonRows } from '../data/comparison';
import { completeFootprintLocationGroups } from '../data/locations';
import { trustBarCards } from '../data/trust';
import { howItWorks, managedForYou, theLineBody } from '../data/brand-lines';

// §0 Metadata. ✅ REWRITTEN against the September 2026 copy-rewrite brief
// (workbook row 18, "Airbnb and short-stay turnovers").
//
// TITLE is the mega-menu's own label (navigation.ts line 383), set on the
// client's instruction, 29 Sep 2026. It is NOT the workbook's approved line.
//
// ⚠️ THIS GIVES THE MAPPED HEAD TERM UP IN THE TITLE TAG. The workbook's line
// existed because `airbnb cleaning services` (200/mo) has to appear
// contiguously, and the title before it split the phrase with "and Holiday
// Let" so the page carried the head term nowhere at all. "Airbnb & Holiday
// Home Cleaning" splits it in exactly the same place, and also drops
// `short stay cleaning`, which the approved line picked up in its modifier.
//
// The phrase is still carried contiguously by the description below, by the
// H1 (§hero `headingLines`) and by the non-branded FAQ heading, so the page is
// not silent on it — but the title tag, which is the strongest of those
// signals, no longer is. If that term's rankings slip, this is the change to
// look at first.
//
// 45 characters. It does gain one thing the approved line did not have:
// the title now matches the menu label a reader clicked to get here.
//
// ⚠️ DESCRIPTION IS *NOT* THE WORKBOOK'S APPROVED LINE, and the divergence is
// deliberate and recorded. The approved meta reads:
//
//   "Airbnb cleaning and same-day turnovers with linen, restocking and a photo
//    report after every changeover. Byron to Palm Beach. Talk to us today."
//
// The brief itself raises three change requests against it and asks for a
// ruling. All three are taken here rather than pasted and left live:
//
//   1. "Byron to Palm Beach" is the coastal cherry-picking BFD §2.1 warns
//      against by name. Those are two towns at opposite ends of the footprint,
//      and describing the service area that way silently excludes the entire
//      inland Northern Rivers and every Tweed town between them — §2.1's own
//      example is that naming only Byron and Ballina "quietly tells somebody
//      in Casino, Murwillumbah or Evans Head that they are not the market".
//      The three regions are named instead, as the old meta already did and as
//      the town band in the render now does.
//   2. "A photo report after every changeover" is a service claim this page
//      does not make. §4's sixth block promises TLB tells the host what it
//      found the same day, which is not the same thing. CUT until somebody
//      confirms photos are standard on every turnover — if they are, the block
//      should say so and the meta can have it back.
//   3. The approved line says "Airbnb cleaning" without "services", so it does
//      not actually contain the mapped phrase. Fixed here: the line now opens
//      on `airbnb cleaning services` contiguously.
//
// 140 characters, and it keeps the approved CTA as its final sentence, which is
// what §7's button label is taken from.

// §1 Hero.
//
// ⚠️ SUPERSEDED NOTE, kept because it records a real disagreement. This used
// to read: "The H1 is the BFD's own approved headline for this audience ('Set
// and forget hosting starts here.') and the CTA is its approved CTA for the
// same block ('Let us handle your turnovers') — not the sitewide 'Get an
// instant quote'. A host is buying an arrangement, not a job, and the CTA
// should say so. The kicker carries the focus keyword, which frees the H1 to
// be a promise rather than a keyword."
//
// The September 2026 copy-rewrite brief overrides both halves, on SEO grounds
// rather than voice grounds, and it is right about the H1: the mapped phrase
// was absent from every heading on the page. The headline is demoted, not cut.
//
// It is more arguable about the CTA, and the brief says so itself: "the live
// CTA is better than the approved one... 'Let us handle your turnovers' is BFD
// §6.5's approved language almost word for word, it names the outcome rather
// than the mechanism, and it speaks to §6.2's insight that a host wants to stop
// thinking about turnovers. 'Talk to us today' is generic and could sit on any
// page on any cleaning site." It is changed anyway, because Rule 4 takes the
// CTA from the final sentence of the approved meta — that is the wording the
// searcher was shown in the result — and because §9.5 wants ONE primary CTA
// repeated, which means all four instances move together or none do.
//
// ⚠️ WORKBOOK CHANGE REQUEST: if row 18 is ever reopened, the better line is
// the one that was already here. Put "Let us handle your turnovers" back into
// the approved meta rather than taking it out of the buttons.
//
// quoteCta.href is still the destination, so there is one form to build, not
// two.
//
// A SCENE PHOTO (family A), not a cut-out figure: the shutters, the ocean
// through them, the squared cushions and the restocked bench are what say
// *holiday let*, and none of them survive a cut-out. So `imageFit` is unset
// and takes Placeholder's `cover` default, and `ratio` is 1/1 because the
// source is 1024×1024 — a box matching the file crops nothing. Regenerate
// square if this is ever reshot, or change both together.
//
// ⚠️ GENERATED, NOT PHOTOGRAPHED. A person who does not exist, in the
// IMAGE-GUIDELINES §3 canonical uniform. Honest as generic service imagery
// and not presented as a named team member, which §7 forbids. A real photo
// of a real TLB cleaner in a real Byron holiday let would do more; treat
// this as a good placeholder rather than the finished job.
// ✅ H1 REWRITTEN (copy-rewrite brief §4). The BFD headline is not cut — it
// moves one line down into the new `subheading` slot, which is what that prop
// was added to Hero.astro for. The exact mapped phrase is scored present or
// absent and was absent from both the H1 and the opening paragraph; the
// extension is what this buyer is actually shopping for, per BFD §6.4 ("working
// straight off the booking calendar") and §6.5's approved "We work to your
// booking calendar, not the other way around."
//
// "Set and forget hosting starts here." is BFD §6.2 in five words — a host is
// perfectly capable of organising a cleaner and the value is in not having to.
// It lands one line lower and loses nothing. The stray double space the live
// markup carried between "hosting" and "starts" is gone with the split.
//
// The KICKER stays as it was: with the H1 now carrying the mapped phrase, the
// eyebrow is where "holiday let" stays visible above the fold, which matters
// for `holiday let cleaning` and for the hosts in this region who do not think
// of themselves as running an Airbnb.
//
// INTRO, as supplied (September 2026): the subheading and lead together read as
// one paragraph. The lead's opener now names all three kinds of property
// ("Airbnb, holiday home and short-term rental") rather than repeating the
// mapped phrase, which the H1 still carries contiguously.

// Trust bar: the shared set, with this page's supplied wording on the client
// count ("...served"). Overridden here only; trust.ts is unchanged.

// §2 Definition. The answer-target block for the focus keyword and the first
// place the phrase appears in body copy. Paragraph two does the synonym work
// ("holiday let turnover", "short-stay cleaning", "changeover") so this one
// page answers all of those queries instead of four thin pages competing for
// them — the same call the commercial carpet page makes with "steam cleaning".

// §2a Short-term rental and holiday home cleaning. Supplied copy (September
// 2026), verbatim. Separates the two service levels the hero's lead now names:
// a turnover for any listing, and whole-property care for owners who live away.

// §2b What we take care of. Supplied copy (September 2026), verbatim. An icon
// row, so it runs on ServiceBlocks' icon-grid like §4. ServiceIcon has no
// camera or wrench glyph; the nearest existing icons stand in.
//
// ⚠️ "Garden and outdoor care / Coming March 2027" is a dated promise on a
// live page. Change it to a plain line (or cut it) when it launches or slips.

// §3 Pathway cards. The three situations a host actually arrives in, taken
// from the BFD's pain-point list for this audience (coordination around
// bookings and same-day turnovers; restocking and presentation; managing it
// all from out of area). Not three services — three readers.
//
// Destinations spread deliberately: the quote flow, the guide the nav
// already promises, and /why-tlb/. A leaf that links only to a quote form
// strands the host who is comparing rather than buying.
//
// ⚠️ Card 2's destination /guides/the-airbnb-turnover-checklist/ is taken
// verbatim from navigation.ts' Guides mega-menu, where it is listed and
// unbuilt. It will 404 until that guide ships. Kept rather than repointed
// because it is the honest destination and the menu already advertises it —
// but if the guides section slips past launch, this card needs the quote
// flow instead. Same open item as every other guide link on the site.
//
// In `pathwayCards`, on "I host from out of town and cannot see the place":
// BFD approved headline for this exact reader: "Hosting from out of
// town? We've got your keys covered."

// §4 What a turnover includes. THE MOST IMPORTANT BLOCK ON THIS PAGE, and
// the reason it is here rather than a paragraph: a host comparing quotes is
// comparing scopes, and the differentiator the BFD claims for this audience
// is precisely that TLB does more than "the basics and leave".
//
// ⚠️ EVERY ITEM IS DRAFTED against the BFD's differentiator list, not
// supplied as page copy by TLB. Confirm each one and CUT anything TLB does
// not actually do. An overstated scope is worse than a short one, because
// this is the block a host will hold us to on a Sunday afternoon.
//
// ✅ Supplied copy (September 2026). The three [Confirm] brackets (calendar
// mechanism, linen, restock billing) are gone from the cards; the header's
// BLOCKS LAUNCH list still stands for the FAQs that carry the same questions.
// Cards 4 and 5 are tagged with the plans they come with.
//
// In `turnoverItems`, on "Essentials restocked":
// BFD names toilet paper and olive oil specifically. Kept, because a
// specific is what makes this believable.

// §4a Choose your level. Supplied copy (September 2026), verbatim. The three
// plans the "Turnover + Restock" and "Full Property Care" tags above refer to.
// Runs on ComparisonTable; empty strings, not `false`, so the gaps render
// blank as supplied rather than as crosses.

// §7a What you get after every stay. Supplied copy (September 2026), verbatim.
// Runs on ServiceBlocks' image-cards with no card links.
//
// Real TLB photos from the team's own shoot (6 Oct 2026), replacing the
// AI-generated stand-ins (prompts still in AFTER-EVERY-STAY-PROMPTS.md). They
// show the team at work rather than literally being a photo report, a restock
// list or a flagged item, so none is captioned as one. The brief's ideal is
// still those real artefacts, with identifying details removed, if they are
// ever supplied. The labels are the alt text and describe each photo.

// §7b Holiday home cleaning for owners who live away. Supplied copy (September
// 2026), verbatim.
//
// ⚠️ ANCHOR IS LOAD-BEARING: interstate ads land on #owners-who-live-away.
// Renaming the id breaks those ads silently, so change it in the ads first.

// §5 "Worth knowing" — the interruption block. Callout, tone="note", the
// same job it does on the NDIS page: correct something the reader probably
// believes before they compare on price.
//
// The lever here is the BFD's own consequence framing for this audience:
// what inaction costs a host is not a dirty house, it is the review. That is
// the one argument a cheaper quote cannot answer.

// §6 Why the same team matters. TextBlock dark, the sibling pages' "worth
// knowing" long-form slot.
//
// This is where the BFD's "first month is the critical proving period" and
// "once a cleaner proves dependable, hosts rarely switch" insight earns its
// place — stated as what the host should demand, not as what TLB claims.

// §7 Before/after. Shared with five other pages, per the
// same-structure-everywhere call.
//
// These frames are REAL TLB JOB PHOTOGRAPHS, published with the client's
// permission and with nothing identifying in frame — no house numbers, street
// signs, mail, people, or names on doors. That is why this section carries no
// "illustrative samples" note. Do NOT wire the `sample-` files back in without
// restoring a note that says so: a fabricated "before and after from a real
// job" is a false trust claim, not filler.
//
// ⚠️ BOTH PAIRS ARE KITCHEN SURFACES. The shower/bathroom pair this section
// used to carry was dropped rather than shown as an illustration next to a
// photograph, so nothing on this page evidences bathroom work. A real shower
// pair is the outstanding shoot; when it lands it takes slot 2 back.
//
// THE PHOTO BRIEF this stands in for, and it is a better brief on this page
// than on any other: a turnover is the one clean where before and after are
// twenty minutes apart, in a property nobody lives in, with no tenant or
// client to consent — so it is by far the easiest place on the site to shoot
// a real pair. One fixed tripod position, nothing moved between frames.
// Kitchen bench and shower screen are the two that read at a glance.
//
// ⚠️ Written permission from the host is still required, and no identifying
// detail in frame — no street numbers, no mail, no guest belongings.

// §8 What we turn over. The property types this work actually covers, as
// pills — the same shape the commercial pages use for premises types.
//
// ⚠️ Every href is a flat kebab-case guess from its label except the four
// that already exist elsewhere in the site (deep, end of lease, mould,
// house cleaning). Unconfirmed sitewide, per navigation.ts.

// §10 Comparison table. The shared approved rows, now on a fifth page.
//
// ⚠️ ONE ROW IS OFF-TARGET HERE and it is worth a decision rather than a
// shrug: "Ongoing contracts with the region's leading real estate agencies"
// answers an agency's question, not a host's. It is the row the NDIS page
// judged wrong for its audience and dropped the whole table over. Kept here
// because for a host it still reads as "this is a real business other
// professionals rely on", and because the trust bar above already makes the
// same claim on every page. If TLB would rather it were host-specific, the
// fix is a page-local `rows` array with a holiday-let row in its place —
// not an edit to the shared file, which four other pages read.
//
// In `comparisonTable`, on "Property checks and trades":
// The shared rows, unchanged, plus one supplied row (September 2026) for
// this page only. Appended here rather than in comparison.ts so the other
// pages' tables are untouched; Full Property Care exists only on this page.

// §11 Branded FAQs — what hosts ask us. Supplied copy (September 2026),
// verbatim: five kept, four new. The kept answers came without their
// [Confirm] brackets (calendar mechanism, same-day cut-off), so those are gone.

// §12 Non-branded FAQs — the long-tail block, aimed at what hosts search
// rather than what they ask us. Supplied copy (September 2026), verbatim: five
// kept, one new. The kept answers came without their [Confirm] brackets
// (pricing basis, linen), so those are gone.
//
// ⚠️ VERIFICATION PASS STILL RECOMMENDED. Two of these make checkable claims
// about things outside TLB's control:
//   - The cleaning-fee answer describes how a fee behaves on a booking
//     platform. Deliberately platform-neutral, because the platforms change
//     their own fee mechanics without notice.
//   - The night-cap answer describes NSW short-term rental planning rules and
//     a shire day cap. See the file header: it needs a compliance read and a
//     "last reviewed" date, or it comes off the page.
//
// In `nonBrandedFaqItems`, on "Are there rules about how many nights I can let my place?":
// ⚠️ COMPLIANCE READ REQUIRED — see the file header. Written without
// dates, precinct names or a day count on purpose: every one of those
// is exactly the kind of specific that goes stale silently and that a
// host may act on. Do not "improve" this by adding them without a
// verification pass and a visible last-reviewed date.

// §13 Closing CTA. Same offer as the hero, worded as what actually happens.
// Kicker matches the hero's, the pattern the commercial leaves set.

// CONTENT lives in src/content/pages/airbnb-holiday-home-cleaning.yaml and is
// edited in Pages CMS ("Airbnb and holiday home cleaning"). This file owns the
// layout. The notes above explain why each section says what it says; they
// stay here because Pages CMS rewrites the YAML on save and would drop them.
//
// The "Talk to us today" buttons and the property-type pills that go to the
// quote form carry its address (/contact/) in the content file, because each
// is a full button an editor can repoint.

// "What a turnover includes" uses two things the shared icon grid does not:
// a plan tag on an item and a button under the grid.
const turnoverGrid = section.iconGrid.extend({
  items: z.array(section.iconGrid.shape.items.element.extend({ tag: section.optional(section.text) })).min(1),
  cta: section.optional(section.link),
});

// The shared comparison rows come from Site settings; only this page's own
// extra rows are in its content file.
const comparisonRow = section.comparisonTable.shape.rows.element;

const page = await getPage(
  'airbnb-holiday-home-cleaning',
  z.object({
    seo: section.seo,
    hero: section.hero,
    // This page's wording on the shared "Over 100 clients" trust bar card.
    trustBar: z.object({ clientsDescription: section.text }),
    // Its background photo sits behind the trust bar and this block together
    // (the Backdrop below), so it cannot be left empty.
    definition: section.textBlock.extend({ backgroundImage: section.backgroundPhoto }),
    shortStay: section.textBlock,
    takeCareOf: section.iconGrid,
    pathways: section.pathwayCards,
    turnover: turnoverGrid,
    levels: section.comparisonTable,
    worthKnowing: section.callout,
    whySameTeam: section.textBlock,
    beforeAfter: section.beforeAfter,
    afterEveryStay: section.imageCards,
    ownersAway: section.textBlock,
    propertyTypes: section.tagCloud,
    comparison: section.comparisonTable
      .omit({ columns: true, rows: true })
      .extend({ extraRows: section.optional(z.array(comparisonRow)) }),
    reviews: section.sectionHeading,
    whereWeClean: section.tagCloud,
    // The paragraphs all come from Brand lines (the "Managed for you" line,
    // The Line's turnover paragraphs and the turnover How it works steps), so
    // only the heading and the optional button and photo are this page's.
    booking: section.textBlock.omit({ body: true }),
    hostFaq: section.faq,
    faq: section.faq,
    closingCta: section.callToAction,
  }),
);

const pageMeta = {
  ...page.seo,
  // ⚠️ Placeholder domain, same as every sibling page. Confirm before launch.
  canonical: 'https://tlbcleaning.com.au/airbnb-holiday-home-cleaning/',
};

const business = localBusiness({
  description: pageMeta.description,
  // BOTH STATES, unlike commercial-cleaning and ndis-cleaning which stop at
  // the border. Deliberate: the BFD's own audience section for short-stay
  // hosts names "the Northern Rivers and Southern Gold Coast" explicitly, and
  // twice. So this page uses the full `locationGroups` below rather than
  // `nswLocationGroups`.
  areaServed: 'Northern Rivers and Tweed NSW, and the Southern Gold Coast QLD',
  sameAs: googleBusinessProfileUrl ? [googleBusinessProfileUrl] : [],
});

const heroImage = page.hero.image && sized(page.hero.image, '1/1');

// Trust bar: the shared set, with this page's own wording on the client count.
const pageTrustBarCards = trustBarCards.map((card) =>
  card.title === 'Over 100 clients' ? { ...card, description: page.trustBar.clientsDescription } : card,
);

const pathwayCards = page.pathways.cards.map((card, i) => ({
  number: i + 1,
  ...card,
  image: sized(card.image, '4/3'),
}));

const afterEveryStayItems = page.afterEveryStay.items.map((item) => ({
  ...item,
  image: item.image && sized(item.image, '4/3'),
}));

const comparisonRowsForPage = [...comparisonRows, ...(page.comparison.extraRows ?? [])];
---

<Base title={pageMeta.title} description={pageMeta.description} canonical={pageMeta.canonical} business={business}>
  <SiteHeader
    logo={{ label: 'TLB Cleaning', src: logoImg }}
    primaryNav={primaryNav}
    serviceNav={headerNav}
    secondaryCta={headerSecondaryCta}
    cta={quoteCta}
  />

  <main id="main">
    <Hero
      variant="split-single-image"
      imagePosition="right"
      kicker={page.hero.kicker}
      headingLines={page.hero.headingLines}
      subheading={page.hero.subheading}
      lead={page.hero.lead}
      cta={page.hero.cta ?? quoteCta}
      image={heroImage}
    />

    {/* Trust bar and definition share ONE photo behind a Cream scrim, laid
        across both sections.

        ⚠️ 90%, NOT the 75% every sibling page uses. This page is deliberately
        the odd one out, changed 29 Sep 2026 on the client's report that this
        block was unreadable.

        The comment that stood here claimed 75% "keeps Dark Teal body copy
        above 4.5:1 over the darkest part of the frame". THAT WAS WRONG, and
        it is worth recording rather than quietly deleting. Measured properly
        — Dark Teal #234B51 over the sRGB composite of Cream #EBE1C7 at 75%
        on the photo beneath — the ratios are:

          near-black shadow   4.07:1   AA FAIL (4.5:1 needed)
          dark clothing       4.44:1   AA FAIL
          the green shirt     5.53:1   passes
          mid window light    6.11:1   passes

        (The same arithmetic reproduces the 7.34:1 that tokens.css states for
        Dark Teal on flat Cream, so the model is not the thing in doubt.)

        At 90% the worst case is 5.89:1 — AA at every size, with headroom.

        Luminance is only half of why this page was the one that got noticed.
        The subject wears a GREEN shirt, and it sits directly behind the copy.
        At 5.53:1 that area passes on paper, but teal ink on a green field is
        a hue clash as well as a contrast one, and the eye reads it as mush
        long before a checker complains. The heavier scrim answers both.

        This page was fixed first, on its own. The rest of the site followed
        the same day: all 48 Backdrop call sites now sit at 90, and the
        per-image measurements live in Backdrop.astro's `opacity` doc. The
        origin of the old 75 — and of the wrong reasoning behind it — is
        recorded at index.astro's §2 comment. */}
